const html = "<section class=\"page-hero\">\n<div class=\"container\">\n  <nav class=\"breadcrumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"/\">Home</a></li><li class=\"sep\" aria-hidden=\"true\">/</li><li><a href=\"/insights\">Insights</a></li><li class=\"sep\" aria-hidden=\"true\">/</li><li aria-current=\"page\">Hiring strategy</li></ol></nav>\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">Insights</p>\n  <h1>Why your niche engineering role has been open for six months</h1>\n  <p class=\"lede\">Four diagnoses, in the order we usually find them. Only one of them is a sourcing problem.</p>\n</div>\n</section>\n<div class=\"cred-strip\">\n  <div class=\"container\">\n    <ul>\n      <li><span class=\"mark\">AUTHOR</span> Smitha C.R.</li>\n      <li><span class=\"mark\">PUBLISHED</span> 5 August 2026</li>\n      <li><span class=\"mark\">LENGTH</span> 6 min read</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<section class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"container\">\n  <div class=\"split split--narrow-first\">\n    <div class=\"reveal\">\n      <p class=\"eyebrow\">Hiring strategy</p>\n      <div class=\"contact-card\">\n        <div class=\"r\">Written by</div>\n        <h3>Smitha C.R.</h3>\n        <p style=\"font-size:var(--text-small)\">Co-Founder, Business &amp; People Solutions</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"prose reveal\" data-delay=\"60\">\n      <p>When a specialist engineering role has been open for six months, the instinct is to conclude that the talent isn't out there. Occasionally that is true. Far more often, the role is unfillable as written rather than unfillable in principle.</p><p>These are the four causes we find, roughly in the order they turn up when we take over a stalled mandate.</p><h3>1. The brief describes two people</h3><p>This is the most common by a wide margin. Somewhere in the requisition process, two adjacent needs got merged into one job description — a deep specialist and a generalist who can also cover the neighbouring function.</p><p>The test is simple: take the essential criteria and ask whether any single person in the market plausibly holds all of them, with the seniority stated, at the band offered. If a hiring manager cannot name three people who fit, the profile is probably a composite rather than a role.</p><p>The fix is not to lower the bar. It is to decide which half of the role is the one that actually needs to be hired for, and to be explicit that the other half will be trained, shared or deferred.</p><h3>2. The compensation band was set against the wrong reference</h3><p>Bands are usually set internally, benchmarked against what the organisation already pays. That is a reasonable way to preserve internal equity and a poor way to predict whether an offer will be accepted.</p><p>In genuinely scarce skill markets the external clearing price moves faster than internal structures do. The signal that this is your problem is specific: candidates engage, interview well, and then decline at offer — or the pipeline converts fine until the number is disclosed, at which point it stops.</p><p>If offers are being rejected rather than interviews being declined, the problem is almost never sourcing.</p><h3>3. The process is longer than the candidate's patience</h3><p>A senior specialist engineer considering a move is usually in two or three processes at once. Whichever organisation reaches a decision first has a structural advantage, independent of how attractive the role is.</p><p>Five interview rounds spread over seven weeks does not select for the best candidate. It selects for the candidate with no other options — which is close to the opposite of what the process is meant to achieve.</p><p>Where a process cannot be shortened, it can at least be made predictable: scheduled in advance, communicated up front, and held to. Candidates tolerate length far better than they tolerate silence.</p><h3>4. The market genuinely is that thin</h3><p>Sometimes the diagnosis really is scarcity. A specific combination — this protocol, that tool, this regulatory regime, at director level, in one city — may describe a population of a few dozen people nationally, most of whom are not moving.</p><p>This is a legitimate answer, but it should be reached by evidence rather than by exhaustion. A talent map will tell you how many people fit, where they are, and what would have to change for any of them to be reachable. That analysis takes weeks. Discovering the same thing through failed search takes quarters.</p><p>And when scarcity is confirmed, the decision moves from recruitment to strategy: relocate the role, restructure it into two, build the capability internally, or pay materially above band for the few people who exist. All four are viable. Continuing to advertise the original role is not.</p><h3>Where to start</h3><p>If a role has been open for six months, we would look at the offer-decline rate before looking at the sourcing channel. Sourcing problems produce empty pipelines. The other three produce full pipelines that go nowhere, which is a much more common and much more expensive failure.</p>\n      <div class=\"note\" style=\"margin-top:var(--s-8)\"><strong>Hiring in this space?</strong> We map these markets for a living. <a href=\"/contact\">Talk to us</a>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"margin-top:var(--s-9)\">\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Related service</p>\n    <div class=\"grid grid--2\" style=\"margin-top:var(--s-5)\"><a class=\"card reveal\" href=\"/services/permanent-recruitment\"><h3>Permanent Recruitment</h3><p>Specialist and niche technical hiring across engineering functions</p><span class=\"link\">Explore <svg class=\"arw\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" fill=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M3 8h9M8.5 4l4 4-4 4\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></svg></span></a></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</section>\n\n<section class=\"cta-band\">\n<div class=\"container\">\n  <div class=\"split\">\n    <div class=\"reveal\">\n      <h2>Let's talk about the team you're building.</h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"reveal\" data-delay=\"80\">\n      <p>Tell us the role, the constraint and the timeline. We'll tell you honestly whether we're the right firm for it &mdash; and if we're not, we'll say so.</p>\n      <div class=\"btn-row\">\n        <a class=\"btn btn--primary\" href=\"/contact\">Start a conversation</a>\n        <a class=\"btn btn--secondary\" href=\"tel:+917760342202\">Call +91 77603 42202</a>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</section>";

